import Agents.Sim.World;

program AgentsExe;

// ONE PASS OVER THE WORLD, and the reason the tick order can be read as a list.
//
// Fourteen classes had the same two methods -- `tick(...)` and `report()` -- and no type said so.
// They could not become objects while each took a different set of world handles, which is what
// `World` fixed: every one of them wants a subset of the same eight, so `tick(World*)` is the shape
// they all fit. What is left in `Sim.tick` is the ORDER, and the order is now a list.
//
// `runsAt` IS WHERE A CADENCE BELONGS. Three of these do not run every tick -- the census every
// twenty years, the settling every sixty ticks, the claim and the crowning on the year -- and until
// now every one of those conditions lived in `Sim.tick` while the constant it read lived in the
// system. `Succession.EVERY_YEARS` is the plainest case: it has been declared in `Succession` since
// the pass existed and nothing in that file has ever read it, because the code that honours it is in
// another class. One fact, two places, and only one of them was ever updated.
//
// `chargedToPeople` IS A MEASURED CONSTRAINT and not a category. What a person costs is the SEVEN
// passes over the crowd, whole -- a partial meter does not merely understate a cost, it points at
// the WRONG one, which this project has paid for twice. The loop opens one stopwatch at the first
// pass that says yes and closes it after the last, so the account stays one bracket and two clock
// reads rather than seven of each.
public bundle Agents {
    public namespace Sim {

        public interface Layer {
            // What it is called, in the chronicle and in a printed order.
            method name() returns String;

            // Whether this pass runs on this tick. Most say yes always; the ones that do not carry
            // their own cadence, which is the point.
            method runsAt(World* now) returns boolean;

            // Do it.
            //
            // `pass` AND NOT `tick`, which the map asked for: every one of these classes keeps a
            // static `tick(...)` with explicit handles, because fifty-three checks call one system
            // with a hand-made world -- which is exactly what a check of one system should do -- and
            // the language has no overloading to let one name serve both. `pass` is this project's
            // own word for the thing anyway: "seven passes, one stopwatch".
            method pass(World* now) returns void;

            // Whether this pass is part of what a PERSON costs. See the note above: the seven that
            // say yes are consecutive, and the account is the whole of them.
            method chargedToPeople() returns boolean;
        }
    }
}
